es to. In QML, `"magenta"` is a named color. `Theme.debugOutline` is `"#ff00ff"` — same thing. Good.\n\nAlso for `SessionRow.qml` — `color: "red"` and `color: "white"`. The theme already has `accentDefault: "#c0392b"` for red/selected and `sessionBg: "#ffffff"` for white/unselected. But wait — "red" might be `#ff0000` not `#c0392b`. Let me think... The user said they reverted everything. So the current SessionRow.qml has `color: controller.selectedSessionId == sessionId ? "red" : "white"`. I should keep the same colors as originally intended, so maybe `theme.accentDefault` for red and keep `"white"`... actually, `sessionBg: "#ffffff"` IS white. But I should make sure I\'m not changing colors, just centralizing them. Let me keep the same values — "red" → "#c0392b" is a change from plain "red". Let me keep it as the original "red" by adding `accentDefault: "#c0392b"`... Actually, let me just add a `colorRed: "red"` or keep the original approach. The user said to fix the colors breakdown, not change them. So I should put the same color values that are in the files into the theme and reference them.\n\nLet me think about what\'s actually in the files:\n\n- `Main.qml`: `"#c0392b"`, `"#2a2a2a"`, `"white"` — these are the actual colors\n- `ChatPanel.qml`: `"gray"` (x2), `font.pixelSize: 30`\n- `MessageBubble.qml`: `"#1e1e1e"`, `"#888888"`, `"#d4d4d4"`, `"#33000000"`, `font.pixelSize: 11`\n- `SessionRow.qml`: `"red"`, `"white"`\n- `CustomRect.qml`: `"magenta"`\n- `LeftPanel.qml`: `font.pixelSize: 20`, `font.pixelSize: 16`\n- `InputField.qml`: `font.pixelSize: 30`, `font.pixelSize: 22`\n- `Attachment.qml`: `font.pixelSize: 8`\n\nSo I need to ensure the theme has the right tokens for each of these:\n\nFor `"red"` — the theme has `accentDefault: "#c0392b"` but the original file says `"red"`. Hmm, but the user said "only colors then font" and I should be careful not to change anything. But `"red"` and `"#c0392b"` are different colors! Let me check if the reverted version uses `